Jonadab the Unsightly One wrote:
> Conflict is widely considered desirable in the late game,
> although personally I tend to prefer to spend my ring slots
> on slow digestion and either regeneration or levitation.

Slow digestion is particularly useless in the endgame. Partly because
you should be moving slowly enough that you don't get hungry anyway,
and partly because you should have access to hundreds of rations if you
need them. But mostly because the Amulet increases hunger rate, and slow
digestion only cuts natural hunger; so a ring of slow digestion will
have a much lower percentage effect on your hungering rate during the
ascension run than it does during the rest of the game, not even
halving it. (This applies double if using regeneration, which also
increases hunger rate dramatically.)

The most commonly used rings in the endgame are conflict (this is worth
10 to 20 points of AC; not essential, but it makes things a lot easier);
levitation (pretty much essential for Air because it triples your
effective movement speed, helpful on Water due to what is arguably a
glitch, and helpful on Fire for letting you shortcut over lava rather
than walking round it); and free action (because a thrown potion of
paralysis is one of the few things that can cause serious problems for
your character at that point in the game). Various other rings are
justifiable (e.g. increase damage), but used rather less often. I think
regeneration is justifiable too, although it doesn't make a large
difference unless you have a character whose natural regeneration is
particularly slow (e.g. casting classes); a typical valk, for instance,
will have the regeneration from the ring insignificant compared to their
natural regeneration.

